entry enrichment
term: Uranium Enrichment
difficulty: intermediate
image: ../../assets/images/terms/Enrichment.webp
related_terms: uranium-235
related_terms: uranium-238
related_terms: uranium-hexafluoride
related_terms: critical-mass
quick_facts: Natural U-235: only 0.72%
quick_facts: Light-water reactor fuel: 3–5% enriched
quick_facts: Weapons-grade: >90% — a long technical leap beyond reactor fuel
quick_facts: Gas centrifuges replaced gaseous diffusion worldwide
quick_facts: Effort measured in Separative Work Units (SWU)

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Uranium Enrichment","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle/enrichment-and-conversion"}

definition:
    Concentrating the fissile uranium-235 fraction above its stingy 0.7% natural abundance. Because isotopes share identical chemistry, separation exploits tiny mass differences: thousands of spinning gas centrifuges feed UF₆ through cascades, each stage nudging the ratio upward. Reactor fuel lands at 3–5%, research reactors may want 20%, and weapons-grade means >90%. The same machines serve both ends — which makes every centrifuge plant a proliferation question.

debunking_snippet:
    “Any country with enrichment can quickly build a bomb.” Reactor-grade (3–5%) to weapons-grade (>90%) is an enormous engineering, energy, and detection challenge — that gap is exactly what IAEA inspectors police.
